1
00:00:00,358 --> 00:00:04,060
This is Kakushi, the privacy provider for Monad.

2
00:00:04,201 --> 00:00:06,437
On Monad every payment is public:

3
00:00:06,477 --> 00:00:09,679
who paid, who got paid, and how much.

4
00:00:09,762 --> 00:00:12,532
Kakushi is for wallets and payment apps that need private

5
00:00:12,572 --> 00:00:14,903
payouts without building the cryptography.

6
00:00:15,570 --> 00:00:18,343
The header reads Monad's block stream live:

7
00:00:18,426 --> 00:00:21,814
proposed to finalized in about six tenths of a second.

8
00:00:22,335 --> 00:00:25,209
Send privately looks up the recipient's registered

9
00:00:25,249 --> 00:00:28,680
meta-address and pays a one-time stealth address.

10
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:32,805
0.01 MON, and the receipt is back in about a second.

11
00:00:33,168 --> 00:00:34,916
Sign-in runs on Privy:

12
00:00:34,956 --> 00:00:37,705
email, Google or any wallet.

13
00:00:37,789 --> 00:00:41,278
Email needs a one-time code, so this recording connects a

14
00:00:41,318 --> 00:00:42,500
browser wallet.

15
00:00:43,379 --> 00:00:45,325
Shield is a zero-knowledge pool.

16
00:00:45,365 --> 00:00:48,750
On Sepolia I deposit 0.001 ETH.

17
00:00:48,790 --> 00:00:52,085
The note is the only way to withdraw, so I copy it.

18
00:00:54,703 --> 00:00:57,495
Withdraw proves the note in the browser, with Noir and

19
00:00:57,535 --> 00:01:01,373
UltraHonk, in about seven seconds, and the relayer pays the

20
00:01:01,413 --> 00:01:07,170
gas. A brand-new address receives the ETH, minus a ten percent fee.

21
00:01:11,879 --> 00:01:13,430
Pools are permissionless:

22
00:01:13,470 --> 00:01:16,007
anyone can open one for any token.

23
00:01:16,047 --> 00:01:19,502
A withdrawal can also pay a stealth address, or call any

24
00:01:19,542 --> 00:01:22,036
contract, bound by the proof.

25
00:01:23,891 --> 00:01:25,755
The bridge is run by Makers.

26
00:01:25,795 --> 00:01:30,028
Maker 1 quotes 0.5 USDC from Monad to Sepolia, and with

27
00:01:30,068 --> 00:01:33,394
Deliver privately, the payout goes to a one-time address

28
00:01:33,434 --> 00:01:35,360
derived from my meta-address.

29
00:01:39,091 --> 00:01:42,650
Three Monad receipts, each under about a second, then Maker

30
00:01:42,690 --> 00:01:46,450
1 pays on Sepolia eleven seconds later. Settled.

31
00:01:50,035 --> 00:01:52,994
If a Maker misses the deadline, anyone can claim.

32
00:01:53,135 --> 00:01:56,821
The watchtower proves the missing payout with a ZK proof and

33
00:01:56,861 --> 00:01:58,799
slashes the Maker's margin.

34
00:01:59,311 --> 00:02:02,305
That path runs end to end on a fork;

35
00:02:02,446 --> 00:02:05,625
no transfer has gone unpaid on testnet.

36
00:02:05,835 --> 00:02:07,745
Makers are the operator side.

37
00:02:07,785 --> 00:02:11,008
The market shows each Maker's routes, margin and payout

38
00:02:11,048 --> 00:02:14,885
times, and in the Maker console an operator posts margin on

39
00:02:14,925 --> 00:02:17,141
Monad and registers routes.

40
00:02:19,370 --> 00:02:22,186
The explorer lists every bridge transfer, with its source,

41
00:02:22,226 --> 00:02:23,896
payout and status.

42
00:02:27,370 --> 00:02:30,557
Payout windows are attested by Kakushi's own runner, using

43
00:02:30,597 --> 00:02:32,844
Chainlink CRE workflow logic.

44
00:02:32,884 --> 00:02:34,659
It is not a DON yet.

45
00:02:35,758 --> 00:02:38,632
Now FHE, which hides how much.

46
00:02:38,672 --> 00:02:41,755
One USDC becomes an encrypted balance.

47
00:02:41,795 --> 00:02:44,936
Revealing it takes one signature, and the value shows in

48
00:02:44,976 --> 00:02:46,437
this tab only.

49
00:02:49,870 --> 00:02:52,152
Pay privately uses both engines:

50
00:02:52,192 --> 00:02:55,867
0.3, encrypted, to a one-time stealth address, with a little

51
00:02:55,907 --> 00:02:58,970
MON forwarded so the recipient can move it.

52
00:03:03,081 --> 00:03:06,420
Cash out asks the KMS for a signed decryption of just that

53
00:03:06,460 --> 00:03:11,064
amount. Today one hosted engine holds the FHE key, and the

54
00:03:11,104 --> 00:03:13,970
KMS signers run in one service.

55
00:03:15,058 --> 00:03:17,843
The recipient's inbox scans with a viewing key.

56
00:03:17,926 --> 00:03:21,845
Monad's RPC returns logs 100 blocks at a time, so a

57
00:03:21,885 --> 00:03:24,240
full-history scan is slow today;

58
00:03:24,381 --> 00:03:26,887
an indexed scan is next.

59
00:03:29,858 --> 00:03:34,079
For developers, the SDK and the KakushiPay React component

60
00:03:34,119 --> 00:03:36,834
bring these payments into any Monad app.

61
00:03:38,535 --> 00:03:42,651
Honest limits: gasless withdrawals are uneconomic on Monad

62
00:03:42,691 --> 00:03:47,295
at today's gas, the Base and OP spokes await funding, and

63
00:03:47,335 --> 00:03:49,922
slashing has only run on a fork.

64
00:03:50,170 --> 00:03:54,460
Kakushi. ZK hides who, FHE hides how much.

65
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:57,636
Try it at kakushi.vercel.app.

